/* Structure of binary tree node
class Node {
    int data;
    Node left;
    Node right;

    Node(int val) {
        data = val;
        left = right = null;
    }
}*/

class Pair{                                                             // node + horizontal distance
    Node node;                                                          // tree node
    int hd;                                                             // horizontal distance from root
    Pair(Node n, int h) { node = n; hd = h; }                           // constructor
}

class Solution {
    public ArrayList<ArrayList<Integer>> verticalOrder(Node root) {     // returns columns left to right

        Queue<Pair> q = new LinkedList<Pair>();                         // BFS queue keeps level order
        Map<Integer, ArrayList<Integer>> mp = new TreeMap<>();          // hd -> nodes, sorted by hd
        q.add(new Pair(root, 0));                                       // root starts at hd 0

        while(!q.isEmpty()){                                            // process all nodes
            Pair p = q.poll();                                          // front of queue
            Node curr = p.node;                                         // current node
            int hd = p.hd;                                              // its hd

            if(mp.containsKey(hd))                                      // column already exists
                mp.get(hd).add(curr.data);                              // append to that column
            else{                                                       // first node of this column

                ArrayList<Integer> al = new ArrayList<Integer>();       // new column list
                al.add(curr.data);                                      // add node value
                mp.put(hd, al);                                         // store column
            }

            if(curr.left != null) q.add(new Pair(curr.left, hd-1));     // left child goes to hd-1
            if(curr.right != null) q.add(new Pair(curr.right, hd+1));   // right child goes to hd+1
        }
        return new ArrayList<>(mp.values());                            // columns in left to right order
    }
}

//////////////////////// DRY RUN /////////////////////////////

Input: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, N, 6]

Tree with hd in brackets

              1 [0]
            /       \
       2 [-1]       3 [1]
       /    \            \
  4 [-2]   5 [0]        6 [2]

Queue order (node, hd)

(1,0) -> (2,-1) -> (3,1) -> (4,-2) -> (5,0) -> (6,2)

Map after all nodes

hd -2  ->  [4]
hd -1  ->  [2]
hd  0  ->  [1, 5]
hd  1  ->  [3]
hd  2  ->  [6]

Output: [[4], [2], [1, 5], [3], [6]]

//////////////////////// QUESTION ////////////////////////////

Vertical Tree Traversal

Given the root of a Binary Tree, find the vertical traversal of the tree starting from the leftmost level to the rightmost level.
Note: If there are multiple nodes passing through a vertical line, then they should be printed as they appear in level order traversal of the tree.

`````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````

Examples:

Input: root = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, N, N, N, 8, N, 9, N, 10, 11, N]

                          1 [0]
                 /                     \
            2 [-1]                     3 [1]
            /     \                  /       \
       4 [-2]    5 [0]          6 [0]       7 [2]
                      \               \            \
                    8 [1]          9 [1]       10 [3]
                    /
               11 [0]

Vertical lines (hd) from left to right

 hd -2  |  hd -1  |     hd 0      |    hd 1    |  hd 2  |  hd 3
  [4]   |   [2]   |  [1,5,6,11]   |  [3,8,9]   |  [7]   |  [10]

Output: [[4], [2], [1, 5, 6, 11], [3, 8, 9], [7], [10]]
Explanation: The diagram above shows the horizontal distances used to print vertical traversal starting from the leftmost level to the rightmost level.

`````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````

Input: root = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, N, 6]

              1 [0]
            /       \
       2 [-1]       3 [1]
       /    \            \
  4 [-2]   5 [0]        6 [2]

Vertical lines (hd) from left to right

 hd -2  |  hd -1  |  hd 0   |  hd 1  |  hd 2
  [4]   |   [2]   |  [1,5]  |  [3]   |  [6]

Output: [[4], [2], [1, 5], [3], [6]]
Explanation: From left to right the vertical order will be [[4], [2], [1, 5], [3], [6]]

`````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````

Constraints:
1 ≤ size of binary tree, node.data ≤ 10^5

